        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Protein supplements build muscle the same way food does: by supplying the protein that resistance training puts to use. The best evidence shows a real but modest benefit, about 0.3 kg of extra fat-free mass on average, and only when the supplement raises total intake toward roughly 1.6 g/kg a day. Past that point, extra protein from any source does little.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            So train first, count your protein second and buy a shake only if there is a gap to fill. When there is, a 25-40 g dose of a certified whey or a well-built plant blend is the cheapest, easiest way to close it, and over months that small edge adds up.
          </p>
